[{"id":"VRCE-1-64-1","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":1,"english":"Forsaking the northern direction the mighty Muni, O Rāma, betaking himself to the Eastern quarter, became engaged in dreadful austerities.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064001","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7e8a121523d37c55bbbd009f07f83381970e625c4181f51c17ef8debea2c9617","compact":"5f5c2231a491d6a52089c7d7b54063c9a82e3aa4a29b1150bb931d941fab964d","nasal":"5f5c2231a491d6a52089c7d7b54063c9a82e3aa4a29b1150bb931d941fab964d"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-2","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":2,"english":"Adopting the high vow of taciturnity for a thousand years, he, O Rāma began to perform the most signal and arduous austerities.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064002","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ce69ef52ae93f4695c665c557ae706b59d4a063059b6aff53493e672530926f0","compact":"54c6d2261602eb389e7346c64af36f596f14b6c3f2d9edd8c419982eb9e81038","nasal":"54c6d2261602eb389e7346c64af36f596f14b6c3f2d9edd8c419982eb9e81038"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-3","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":3,"english":"When the thousand years had been complete, many an impediment tried the mighty Muni staying like the trunk of a tree, yet could not anger enter his heart.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064003","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7b83c64646d998dcd78db5f4ee8fbfded6268917c8a6d1fd82dcad2b51630645","compact":"4c205f51255e5a61e76e64c8ef49131f450401e4581def24eb9a1f9b019c091a","nasal":"ca8364cb9f8c3069539cf0adae6885c495d65d26ef82ebec5519faa068cdf6f2"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-4","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":4,"english":"And at this, the three worlds became afflicted with fear. And bewildered on account of the energy of his asceticisin, and shorn of their brightness, and afflicted with anguish, the Devarsis and the Gandharvas and the Pannagas and the Uragas and the Rakşasas in a body addressed the Pitāmaha, (Brahmā).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.593,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064004","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"065aa708caee1239636fb9859ec5a893ba98beb2cf7c5bc3c49072f4201388e0","compact":"9772cd6e19cfa148603fa715a1c273447eaaec10ab25dcfa30a388bfc85cb353","nasal":"7f803fae0de2e1fae87fdbc66974f28c9bb105142dbcf16009f5baffa0f55d31"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-5","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":5,"english":"O divine one, various were the means by which we endeavoured to affect the mighty Muni Viśvāmitra with covetousness and lust; but for all that he increase in asceticism.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064005","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dc6699cb1d72a254e7d881bdd5e2bebdd1ae32bcf935d4c9868906729b795488","compact":"4371a797446adb6ed0e6d32a51dfd74a11ac4254d78ec2d726dd7a1d3dcd7cf3","nasal":"4371a797446adb6ed0e6d32a51dfd74a11ac4254d78ec2d726dd7a1d3dcd7cf3"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-6","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":6,"english":"Nor do we perceive in him ever so little of anger or lust. And if you do not confer upon him what his mind desire to have, he will annihilate the three worlds with all that is mobile and immobile in them. And the ten cardinal points are disconsolate: and nothing can be discovered therein.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.938,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064006","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"019056f76e9c0e987102ad2b9ad430bcee04df6c2ccd08bc6a8b642b0cd63d2d","compact":"717ca632dd70b0cc253c82672d805e8335e3eb648291880ef502534919af0f8f","nasal":"717ca632dd70b0cc253c82672d805e8335e3eb648291880ef502534919af0f8f"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-7","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":7,"english":"And the seas are vexed, and the mountains, riven. And the earth shake, and the winds keep steadily blowing.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.545,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064007","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"da8060223ea8d67f2d5d385d0ab57df3c969ce9bb711104dab0b24982865fcef","compact":"7a78d3e7648ddf5bb55b1aa6ad41e72fb33e5f6165a52bbcd3a12cdfb31eeef0","nasal":"450b00839afe36820b53aa72529f712bf34217694ab9236db5c28478ab24d20b"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-8","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":8,"english":"By virtue of that mighty saint's energy, the sun itself has been deprived of his splendour.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.703,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-65-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1064008","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"98582809c66e42f73e7618cf61683c19adf1d9ff61b1ef0fb1e0e5997984183b","compact":"7105f540f4c9fa11a1ab2366a0d8a882059f48110cec58988993497cbefdd730","nasal":"574404a39438625ecfdc20cd9659e5f67ff5d5cd53a7956db8d80ec0e1d05723"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-64-9","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":64,"verse":9,"english":"Therefore, O 